# Contributing

Contributions are welcome. This guide explains how to propose and submit changes; project roles, decision-making, and standards approval are defined in [GOVERNANCE.md](GOVERNANCE.md).

## Before contributing

- Read the [Code of Conduct](CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md).
- Check existing issues and discussions before starting substantial work.
- For a new standard or a substantive change, follow the proposal and lifecycle process described in [Standards Lifecycle](docs/governance/standards-lifecycle.md).

## Submit a change

1. Create a fork or a short-lived branch from `main`, following the [Branching Strategy](docs/contributing/branching-strategy.md).
2. Keep the change focused and explain its purpose. For specification changes, identify affected sections and whether the change is normative or editorial.
3. Open a pull request against `main`, linking any related issue or proposal.
4. Respond to review feedback and update the pull request as needed. See the [Review Process](docs/contributing/review-process.md).

Use the relevant files in [`docs/templates/`](docs/templates/) when a proposal, specification, governance decision, or meeting record needs a consistent starting point.

## Standards approval

A pull request review or merge does not, by itself, constitute formal approval or publication of a standard. Follow the authority and approval process in [GOVERNANCE.md](GOVERNANCE.md).

## Writing and licensing

Write clearly, distinguish requirements from informative guidance, and use requirement keywords such as MUST, SHOULD, and MAY only as defined by the project. Record material changes in the [Changelog](CHANGELOG.md).

Contributions are subject to the project's licensing terms and contribution policy. See [Licensing](docs/governance/licensing.md); raise licensing concerns before submitting a contribution.
